# Line endings for files whose bytes are part of a contract.
#
# core.autocrlf is true on the Windows checkout and unset on the Linux server,
# so the same commit lands as CRLF here and LF there. For most source that is
# harmless. For Prisma migrations it is not: `prisma migrate` records a sha256
# of each migration file when it applies it, so a file that arrives with
# different line endings than it had at apply time reads as "modified after it
# was applied", and every subsequent `migrate dev` offers to reset the
# database instead.
#
# That cost an afternoon twice. Pinning these to LF makes the working tree
# match what the server records, which is the only checksum that matters.
*.sql text eol=lf
*.prisma text eol=lf

# Shell scripts and Ansible templates are executed on Linux; a CR at the end of
# a shebang line or a `fi` is a syntax error there, not a cosmetic difference.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.j2 text eol=lf

# Markdown content is read by the blog parser, which normalises CRLF itself —
# see src/content/blog.ts, which had to learn that the hard way. Pinned anyway
# so the parser's normalisation is a belt rather than the only thing holding
# it up.
*.md text eol=lf
